            Every candidate had to be a real, currently sold product with broad availability, and it had to report in picocuries per liter, the unit US guidance uses. We ruled out cheap no-name monitors whose sensors could not be identified and whose readings wandered by several pCi/L between identical units. A radon detector that is wrong in either direction is worse than none: too high and you spend thousands on mitigation you did not need, too low and you stop worrying about the leading cause of lung cancer in non-smokers.
          </p>

