            The monitors shared a shelf in the same unfinished basement, set at the height and distance from walls that testing protocols call for, and ran together for several weeks. We looked at how long each took to settle into a stable figure, how closely they agreed with one another once they had a few days of data, and how closely they agreed with a lab-analysed charcoal kit run alongside them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then we weighed the things that decide whether a detector gets used after the first month. Can you read the number without an app? Does it run on batteries so you can put it in the right spot? Will it warn someone who never checks it? Those practical factors separate the picks more than raw accuracy does, because every monitor on this list is accurate enough for a homeowner to make a sound decision.
