        <div id="radon-basics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What the Numbers Mean: 4 pCi/L and 2 pCi/L
          </h2>

          <p>
            Radon is a radioactive gas released as uranium in soil and rock breaks down. It seeps into houses through slab cracks, floor-wall joints, sump pits, utility penetrations and bare earth in crawl spaces, and it accumulates in the lowest, most closed-up parts of the house. You cannot see it, smell it or feel it, and the health risk comes from breathing it for years, which is why the measurement that matters is an average rather than a peak.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">THE NUMBERS THAT MATTER</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4.0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">pCi/L: EPA action level, fix the house</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2.0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">pCi/L: EPA says consider fixing</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1.3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">pCi/L: average US indoor level</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.4</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">pCi/L: typical outdoor level</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The EPA action level is 4 pCi/L. If your long-term average is at or above it, the agency recommends fixing the house. It also says homeowners should consider mitigation between 2 and 4 pCi/L, because there is no known safe level of radon and the risk scales with exposure. For comparison, the World Health Organization sets its reference level at 100 becquerels per cubic meter, which works out to about 2.7 pCi/L.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those thresholds are why this guide keeps returning to averages. A house that reads 6 pCi/L during a January cold snap and 2 pCi/L in a breezy May may average right around the action level. Reacting to the January number alone might send you into a mitigation you did not strictly need; relying on the May number might talk you out of one you did.
          </p>

          <p>
            The EPA estimates that roughly one in fifteen US homes has an elevated level, and there is no reliable way to predict which ones from the outside. Neighbours on the same street regularly get very different results, and new construction is not exempt. Testing is the only way to know, which is the whole case for owning a detector.
          </p>
        </div>

