          <p>
            Kits win on price for a single test and are lab analysed, which gives them a certain authority. Their weakness is time and logistics. A two-to-three-day kit only captures those days, and every day it spends in the mail after you seal it lets radon decay inside the charcoal and makes the result less precise. If you test once and get a borderline number, you are back to buying another kit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Monitors win on everything that happens after the first test. They keep running through seasons, they show you whether a number is climbing or settling, and after mitigation they tell you within days whether the system is doing its job. Over two or three years, a monitor usually costs less than the stream of kits you would need to get the same information.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our practical advice: if you have never tested, a kit is a fine first step. If it comes back above 2 pCi/L, or if you plan to live in the house for years, buy a monitor and let it run. And if you are buying or selling a house, neither is a substitute for a test by a certified measurement professional, which is what lenders and contracts usually require.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="averaging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Short-Term vs Long-Term Averages
          </h2>

          <p>
            Radon guidance splits tests into short-term, meaning anything from two to ninety days, and long-term, meaning more than ninety days. The distinction exists because radon swings. Soil gas pressure, wind, rain, frozen ground, the stack effect of warm air rising through a heated house and even exhaust fans all push more or less radon indoors from one day to the next.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winter is usually the high season. With windows shut, the furnace running and warm air escaping through the upper floors, the basement is under slight negative pressure and draws soil gas in through every gap. Summer, with windows open and less temperature difference between inside and out, often reads lower. A short-term test in either season can mislead in opposite directions.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Decide on the long-term average, not on a single day.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 24-hour reading is for curiosity. A 7-day average is a first read. A 90-day or longer average, ideally one that includes the heating season, is the figure the 4 pCi/L action level was written for.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The EPA's decision path reflects this. If a short-term test comes back at 4 pCi/L or higher, it recommends a follow-up test before acting: either a second short-term test, averaged with the first, or a long-term test. If the long-term result or the average of the two short-term tests is 4 or above, fix the house. A continuous monitor makes that path easy because it produces both kinds of average without you buying anything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, that means reading a monitor's display selectively. Ignore the 24-hour value unless you are diagnosing a specific event. Watch the 7-day value in the first few weeks to see which side of 2 and 4 you are on, and make decisions on the long-term value once it covers at least three months. If you can, let it run through a winter before you conclude you do not have a problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where to Place a Radon Detector
          </h2>

          <p>
            Placement changes readings more than the choice of detector does. The standard guidance is to test on the lowest level of the house that is lived in or could reasonably be used as living space. In most homes that is the basement, finished or not, because that is where family members spend time and where radon concentrations are usually highest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set the detector at least 20 inches above the floor, which keeps it in the breathing zone and away from the stagnant layer right at slab level. Keep it about a foot from exterior walls and at least three feet from exterior doors, windows and vents, because drafts dilute radon and give you a falsely low figure. Avoid kitchens, bathrooms and laundry areas, where exhaust fans and humidity interfere, and keep it out of direct sun and away from heat sources such as furnaces and water heaters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not put it right next to a sump pit, floor drain or obvious crack. Those are entry points and will read high, which is useful for diagnosis but not representative of the air you breathe. If you want to find where radon is entering, a fast monitor like the RadonEye can be moved around for a few hours at a time, but your baseline measurement should be taken in a central, typical spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            For short-term tests, keep the house closed for at least 12 hours before and throughout the test, with windows and exterior doors shut except for normal coming and going. That mimics winter conditions and gives a conservative result. For a long-term monitor you simply live normally, since the long average is meant to reflect real life.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once you know the basement figure, it is worth moving the detector to a ground-floor bedroom for a few weeks. Bedrooms are where people spend the most continuous hours, and seeing how much the level drops between floors tells a mitigation contractor a lot about how radon moves through your house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calibration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Accuracy and Calibration
          </h2>

          <p>
            Consumer radon monitors use one of two sensing methods. Airthings models use a passive diffusion chamber with alpha spectrometry, which is accurate but counts slowly, so the first value appears after a day and precision improves as data accumulates. Ecosense and Safety Siren use ion chambers; Ecosense in particular counts fast enough to show a meaningful number within about an hour. Both approaches work, and both get more trustworthy the longer they run.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every detector here is calibrated at the factory, and you cannot recalibrate one at home the way you would a hygrometer. What you can do is cross-check. Once every year or two, run a lab-analysed charcoal kit next to your monitor under closed-house conditions and compare the kit's result with the monitor's average for the same days. Agreement within about 20 to 25 percent at typical residential levels is normal; a larger gap on a repeat test is a reason to contact the manufacturer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep expectations realistic at low concentrations. At 1 pCi/L, a detector that reads 1.4 is off by 40 percent but the difference is meaningless for any decision. Precision specs are usually quoted at 10 pCi/L for that reason. What matters is that a monitor reliably tells a 1.5 house from a 4.5 house, and every pick here does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Humidity and temperature matter at the extremes. Most consumer monitors are rated for roughly 32 to 104 degrees Fahrenheit and relative humidity below about 80 to 85 percent. A dripping basement outside that range can affect readings and the electronics themselves, which is one more reason to know your basement humidity; our <a href="/best-hygrometer/">hygrometer guide</a> covers inexpensive ways to measure it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="acting-on-results" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Acting on Your Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you have a long-term average, the decision tree is short. Below 2 pCi/L, you are in good shape; keep the monitor running or retest every couple of years and after any major renovation, since new additions, finished basements and changes to HVAC can change how radon moves. There is nothing to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between 2 and 4 pCi/L, the EPA says to consider mitigation, and this is where judgment comes in. Consider how many hours people spend on the lowest level, whether anyone smokes, since smoking and radon compound each other's risk sharply, and how long you plan to stay. Many owners in this band start with cheap measures such as sealing obvious openings and improving basement ventilation, then watch the monitor to see whether they helped.
          </p>

          <p>
            At or above 4 pCi/L, fix the house. That is not a panic threshold; the risk comes from years of exposure, so you have time to get quotes and hire properly. But it is a clear recommendation, and the fix is reliable and permanent enough that there is little reason to delay.
          </p>

