        <div id="mitigation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Mitigation Actually Involves
          </h2>

          <p>
            The standard fix for a house with a basement or slab is active sub-slab depressurization. A contractor cores a hole through the slab, digs out a small pit beneath it, and runs a sealed PVC pipe from that pit to a fan that vents above the roofline. The fan keeps the soil under the house at slightly lower pressure than the basement, so soil gas is pulled into the pipe and exhausted outside instead of seeping indoors. Properly installed systems commonly cut indoor radon dramatically, often by well over half and frequently to under 2 pCi/L.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crawl spaces get a variation called sub-membrane depressurization: a heavy plastic vapor barrier is sealed over the bare earth and the fan draws from beneath it. Houses with a sump pit can often use a sealed sump lid as the suction point. Costs vary with the house and region, but most homeowners pay in the low thousands of dollars, which is comparable to other routine home repairs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SEALING IS NOT MITIGATION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sealers help a system work; they rarely fix radon on their own.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The EPA does not recommend sealing alone as a radon fix. Concrete has too many hidden pathways. Sealing cracks and the floor-wall joint does make a depressurization system more efficient by reducing the air it pulls from the basement.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            That callout matters because sealers are heavily marketed for radon. Penetrating products like RadonSeal densify concrete and can reduce some soil gas movement, and our <a href="/drylok-vs-radonseal/">Drylok vs RadonSeal comparison</a> explains where each belongs. Sealing is a worthwhile companion to a fan system and a reasonable first step in the 2 to 4 pCi/L range, especially alongside a proper <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">basement wall sealer</a> for moisture. Just measure before and after, rather than assuming it worked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hire a contractor certified by the National Radon Proficiency Program or the National Radon Safety Board, and retest within about 30 days of installation. Keep your monitor running afterward: a fan that fails, a pipe that cracks or a new crack in the slab shows up as a rising average long before anyone would otherwise notice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-basement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Radon and the Rest of the Basement
          </h2>

          <p>
            Radon rarely travels alone. The same cracks, open sump pits and porous block walls that admit soil gas also admit moisture, which is why a basement with a radon problem often has a humidity or seepage problem too. Treating them as one project usually saves money, because the contractor sealing the slab for a mitigation system can address the floor-wall joint and visible cracks at the same time.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the basement is damp, fix the water first or at least in parallel. A mitigation fan does move some moisture out from under the slab, but it is not a waterproofing system. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-waterproof-a-basement/">how to waterproof a basement</a> walks through grading, gutters, crack repair and interior drainage in the order that actually matters, and it is worth reading before anyone cores a hole in your slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Air purifiers deserve a clear word here. A HEPA purifier captures particles, including some of the solid radon decay products that attach to dust, but it does nothing about radon gas itself and will not lower the reading on your detector. A purifier like the ones in our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/">best air purifier for home</a> roundup is worth owning for allergens, smoke and dust; it is not a radon strategy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, humidity and radon readings together tell a better story than either alone. Several of the monitors here log both, and if yours does not, a cheap hygrometer beside it will. When both rise together during closed-up weeks, you are looking at the same soil-gas pressure driving both, and that is useful information for whoever designs your fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reacting to the 24-hour number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Radon can double overnight when the weather turns cold and fall by half when it warms up. The 24-hour reading on a monitor is a curiosity. Wait for at least a 7-day average before drawing any conclusion, and base spending decisions on a long-term average that covers three months or more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing in the wrong place</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Detectors on the floor, beside a sump pit, next to an open window or in a laundry room produce numbers that do not represent the air you breathe. Place the detector 20 inches or more above the floor on the lowest lived-in level, away from exterior walls, drafts, heat and humidity sources.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mailing a charcoal kit late</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Charcoal kits keep absorbing and radon keeps decaying after the test ends. Leaving a sealed kit on the counter for a week before posting it degrades the result, sometimes enough to be rejected. Seal it and mail it the same day, and avoid starting a test right before a weekend or holiday.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating sealer as a radon fix</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Crack sealers and penetrating coatings can reduce some soil gas entry, but concrete has too many hidden pathways for sealing alone to reliably bring a high reading down. Use sealing as a companion to sub-slab depressurization, and always confirm the effect with a monitor rather than assuming.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing once and never again</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single low test years ago says little about the house today. Renovations, finished basements, new HVAC equipment and settling slabs all change radon entry. Retest every couple of years, after any major work, and within 30 days of installing a mitigation system to confirm it performs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a safe radon level in a home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No level of radon is considered completely safe, because risk rises with exposure. The EPA action level is 4 pCi/L, and it recommends considering mitigation between 2 and 4 pCi/L. The average US indoor level is about 1.3 pCi/L and outdoor air is around 0.4 pCi/L, so a long-term average under 2 is a reasonable target.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a radon detector run before I trust it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A continuous monitor gives a usable first read after about a week, but decisions should rest on a long-term average of at least 90 days. Radon changes with weather and season, and the heating months usually read highest. Letting a monitor run through a full winter gives you the most honest picture of your annual exposure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are home radon detectors accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Reputable consumer monitors are accurate enough to tell a house that needs mitigation from one that does not. They are factory calibrated and most quote precision around 10 percent at 10 pCi/L. Cross-check yours with a lab-analysed kit every year or two. They are not accepted for real estate transactions, which require a certified measurement professional.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where is the best place to put a radon detector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On the lowest level that is lived in or could be, usually the basement. Keep it at least 20 inches off the floor, about a foot from exterior walls and three feet from doors, windows and vents. Avoid kitchens, bathrooms, laundry rooms, sump pits and spots near furnaces, water heaters or direct sun.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a radon test kit or a digital monitor better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A kit is cheaper for a single first screening and is lab analysed. A digital monitor is better for making decisions, because it builds a long-term average, shows trends and confirms whether mitigation works. If a kit comes back above 2 pCi/L, or you plan to stay in the house for years, a monitor is the better investment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can opening windows lower radon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, temporarily. Ventilation dilutes radon and reduces the pressure difference that pulls soil gas indoors, and a fast monitor will show the drop within hours. It is not a practical long-term fix in cold or hot weather, though, and levels rebound as soon as the house is closed. Sub-slab depressurization is the permanent solution.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a radon detector if my house has a mitigation system?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a good idea. Fans fail, pipes crack and slabs settle, and none of that is obvious without a measurement. Most systems include a manometer that shows whether the fan is creating suction, but a running monitor confirms the result that actually matters: that the long-term average stays well below 4 pCi/L.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

